plan_format — implementation plan
=================================

This is the bootstrap plan for replacing convention-only prose plans with one
machine-readable record that remains comfortable to review as Markdown.  Its
claims now live only in the v1 header; the body explains their context without
redeclaring them.

goal
----

Every new or changed plan has one YAML frontmatter document containing its
identity, lifecycle, dependencies, affected modules and invariants, labelled
claims, refinement state, and completion command.  Markdown following the
frontmatter explains context, alternatives, implementation sequence, and
consequences without redefining those fields.

bootstrap completion
--------------------

These five delivery plans form one bootstrap train.  Implement them in the
declared dependency order while their statuses remain planned; do not claim an
individual plan done merely because its module-level tests pass.  The
`planning_gate` plan completes the shared command, after which that command
must pass before any of the five transitions to done.  This is the only
temporary exception to requiring an already-complete dependency before work on
its dependent begins.

format decision
---------------

The canonical file remains an extensionless Markdown plan under `plans/`.
Frontmatter is delimited by `---`, parsed with `yaml.safe_load`, and validated
against a versioned JSON Schema.  The planning tool installs a duplicate-key
rejecting loader because ordinary YAML "last key wins" behavior would make a
reviewed value differ from the value consumed by the gate.

The host-side planning tool owns Python dependencies.  It may invoke Flatpak
Isabelle commands, but Isabelle's Python runtime does not need PyYAML,
jsonschema, or pytest.

The v1 header contains, at minimum:

    schema: isabelle-mcp.plan/v1
    id: planning_gate
    title: Enforce plan evidence and completion
    status: planned
    depends_on: [plan_label_schema, verification_matrix, python_e2e]
    allowed_modules: [tools/planning_gate]
    claims: [...]                 # canonical A/I/D/Q/T records
    spec_refinements: []
    done_command: [tools/planning-gate, done]

Commands are argument arrays, never shell strings.  IDs are stable addresses;
renaming a file or ID requires an explicit migration rather than silently
minting a new address.

implementation sequence
-----------------------

1. Establish `tools/planning_gate/` as a Python package and add a plan-document
   loader whose entry point accepts an explicit repository root.  No module
   parses CLI arguments at import time.
2. Add `plans/schema/plan-v1.schema.json`, pinned to one supported JSON Schema
   dialect, plus semantic validation for cross-file facts that JSON Schema
   cannot decide: unique plan IDs, dependency resolution, and allowed paths.
3. Teach the loader to reject missing/duplicate frontmatter, duplicate YAML
   keys, unknown schema versions, unsafe YAML values, malformed IDs, absolute
   paths, shell-string commands, and unknown top-level keys.
4. Support current prose plans as a read-only legacy input during migration.
   New files must be v1.  A modified legacy plan must either migrate or carry a
   review-visible compatibility exception.
5. Migrate this plan and the four dependent delivery plans first.  Generate the
   legacy `plans/ASSUMPTIONS` and Isabelle ID table from v1 claims so existing
   test citations remain valid.
6. Move remaining plans when changed; do not perform an unreviewed mechanical
   rewrite of every historical plan in this change.
7. Document which fields are normative.  A gate rejects a labelled claim that
   is redefined by a second legacy-style declaration in the Markdown body.

non-goals
---------

- Rewriting the narrative body of historical plans.
- Adding a general YAML templating or code-execution facility.
